Replay fixtures for TestARCH_TEST_002_Replay (ARCH-TEST-002)
==============================================================

Each <name>.bin is the raw output of a program, recorded in an 80x24
terminal with TERM=xterm-256color and LANG=en_US.UTF-8. <name>.golden is
the screen the emulator shows after the whole recording (text without
styles or trailing spaces, then the cursor, alternate screen and mouse
mode), and <name>.sgr.golden the same lines with their SGR styles (ESC
written as \e). The test feeds every recording in 1-byte, 7-byte and
4 KiB chunks, and each chunking must give the goldens.

  vim-edit     vim -u NONE -i NONE -N main.go: search, change a word, open
               a line with CJK text, :syntax on, :set number
  htop         htop -p 1 (only launchd/init, so no user processes)
  less-search  less words.txt, /needle, n
  tmux-split   tmux -f tmux.conf (status-right "", default-command
               /bin/sh): a vertical and a horizontal split, a coloured
               printf
  utf8-wide    sh utf8-wide.sh: double-width characters at and next to the
               right margin (D-015)

Recording
---------

On a real host, from a terminal resized to 80x24:

    ssh -t host 'stty cols 80 rows 24; TERM=xterm-256color script -q /tmp/vim-edit.bin vim -u NONE -i NONE -N main.go'

then use the program as listed above and quit it. Copy the .bin back and
trim it: cut everything after the last frame you want in the golden (for
example the exit sequence of vim), so the recording ends with the program
still on screen. Recordings must not show user names, host names, paths
or anything else private: use -p 1 for htop, an empty status-right for
tmux and files in a scratch directory.

The fixtures in this directory were made the same way, without sshd in
between (which passes the bytes through unchanged): each program ran
under a small creack/pty recorder that opens an 80x24 PTY, sets TERM,
LANG and an empty HOME, types the keys with short pauses and saves
everything the program wrote, stopping the program before it exits.

After changing a fixture or the renderer, rewrite the goldens and review
the diff:

    go test ./internal/session -run Replay -update

Known limitation
----------------

x/vt v0.1.0 finishes a grapheme cluster at the end of every write, so a
combining mark, a ZWJ sequence or a flag split across two network reads
is drawn as separate cells. The fixtures therefore hold no such clusters;
sshelf splits large reads only after control characters, which end every
cluster.
